{
  "requirement": "In Markdown, escaped roles and role-looking text in inline code, fenced code, or raw HTML MUST remain literal and MUST NOT count as references. Raw HTML means an HTML block, an HTML comment, or an inline HTML tag including its attributes; text between inline tags is ordinary Markdown. A `{req}` directive inside a fenced code example and a legacy inline `{req}` role MUST NOT define a requirement.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "Each obligation in `requirements.references.markdown_literals` maps to at least one marked test with real assertions.\n\n**Escaped roles stay literal and are not references.**\n- `src/lib.rs#mod[tests]/fn[markdown_examples_and_escaped_references_stay_literal]` checks the escaped `\\{impl}` role. The marker text survives unchanged, and no `habeas-req-ref-impl` badge is rendered.\n- The same test covers the double-backslash boundary: `reference_roles` returns one role, at offset 2, so an escaped backslash does not escape the role.\n- `src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[every_markdown_extension_keeps_escaped_and_code_roles_literal]` and `src/terms.rs#mod[tests]/fn[term_dependencies_of_every_markdown_extension_skip_literal_roles]` do the same for source references and `{term}` dependencies. They run over `.md`, `.markdown`, `.MD` and `.Markdown` files, and `.txt` is the contrast case.\n\n**Role-looking text in inline code or fenced code stays literal.**\n- Inline code and fenced code are covered by the same tests: `markdown_examples_and_escaped_references_stay_literal`, `every_markdown_extension_keeps_escaped_and_code_roles_literal`, and `references_in_requirement_bodies_are_scanned_as_markdown`.\n- In `references_in_requirement_bodies_are_scanned_as_markdown`, a fenced `{cite}` inside a requirement body is ignored. The real citation in the same body is found exactly once.\n- `src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[source_references_are_covered_by_evidence_inputs]` checks that a role in inline code and in a fenced block in notes is not counted as a reference. The evidence passes without covering the literal `missing.example`.\n- The `{term}` variants are covered in `src/terms.rs` and `tests/definitions.rs#fn[literal_term_roles_in_subject_and_requirements_inputs_need_no_definition]`. That test also has a positive control: the same role written as a real reference does require a definition.\n\n**Raw HTML.** The requirement names three forms, and each is tested.\n- `src/lib.rs#mod[tests]/fn[roles_in_raw_html_stay_literal]` covers an HTML block, an HTML comment, and an inline tag's attribute.\n- The same test shows that text between inline tags (`<em>…</em>`) is ordinary Markdown: it is counted and badged.\n- It also checks the non-Markdown contrast, where all 5 roles count.\n- `src/fenced.rs#mod[tests]/fn[markdown_fences_in_raw_html_stay_literal]` does the same for colon fences inside a block, a comment, and a multi-line tag attribute. Only the fence outside raw HTML is a reference, and plain text counts all of them.\n- `src/lib.rs#mod[tests]/fn[citation_metadata_preserves_prose_other_roles_and_literal_contexts]` adds literal-context cases for `<div>`, `<!-- -->` and `<span>` around a citation.\n\n**A `{req}` directive inside a fenced code example does not define a requirement, and a legacy inline `{req}` role does not either.**\n- `src/lib.rs#mod[tests]/fn[code_examples_and_legacy_roles_remain_literal]` checks that an indented `:::{req} block` inside a fence stays literal. It also checks that `{req}`legacy`` and the inline-code form stay literal, while a real `:::{req} visible` still renders as a definition.\n- `colon_directives_render_markdown_bodies_and_legacy_roles_stay_literal` repeats the legacy-role case. It asserts that no `habeas-req-requirements.inline` anchor is produced, while the real directive is defined.\n- Per an earlier assessment's finding, which I did not recheck, `definition_blocks` is the single parser shared by rendering, evidence loading and term scanning. Testing it through `replace_definitions` therefore covers every definition path.\n\nThe tests assert both sides: the literal case is not counted, and a control case is. A wrongly counted reference or a wrongly defined requirement would fail them. I found no gap, and I found no ambiguity that leads to different conclusions.",
